{% set local_deep_dive %}
<p>Toney sits northwest of Huntsville along Highway 53, in the part of Madison County that still feels like farmland. If you're near Nick Davis Road, out toward the Limestone County line, or anywhere in the <a href="/recent-projects/">Toney school district</a>, you're in our regular service area. We're 15 miles away — that's a 20-minute drive.</p>

<p>This is agricultural country. Properties here often have history — farmhouses that have been in families for generations, barns and outbuildings that have seen decades of Alabama weather. The homes built here weren't designed with modern drainage standards in mind. Many have gutters that were added later, sometimes by previous owners doing the best they could with what they had.</p>

<p>We see a lot of patch jobs in Toney. Sectional gutters held together with gutter seal and hope. Downspouts that empty onto splash blocks that disappeared into the grass years ago. Creative solutions that worked for a while but aren't working anymore.</p>

<p>The agricultural roots also mean larger properties with more roof area to manage. A farmhouse with a wraparound porch, an attached garage, and a couple of outbuildings can have 300+ linear feet of roofline needing drainage. That's real water volume during Alabama storms.</p>

<p>We understand properties like these. We're not going to show up expecting a tidy suburban lot with a simple roofline. We'll assess what you actually have, work with the character of your property, and design a system that handles the water without fighting your home's architecture.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set homeowner_guide %}
<p>Practical advice for Toney property owners:</p>

<p><strong>Don't forget outbuildings.</strong> Barns, workshops, and storage buildings have roofs too. Unmanaged runoff from outbuildings can create drainage problems that affect the whole property — including your house.</p>

<p><strong>Rural lots drain differently.</strong> Without curbs and storm drains, water goes wherever gravity takes it. Walk your property after a heavy rain. Where does water collect? Where does it flow? That information helps design effective drainage.</p>

<p><strong>Tree debris is constant.</strong> Toney's wooded character means gutters fill fast. Options:</p>
<ul>
<li>Gutter guards (best for "set and forget")</li>
<li>Professional cleaning 2-3 times per year</li>
<li>DIY cleaning if you're comfortable on ladders</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Older homes need assessment.</strong> Before installing new gutters on an older farmhouse, we check the fascia. Decades of water exposure often means hidden rot. Better to discover and address it during gutter work than to install new gutters on failing wood.</p>

<p><strong>Seasonal timing:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>March:</strong> Pre-storm season check</li>
<li><strong>November:</strong> Fall cleaning after leaf drop</li>
</ul>

